**Decoding a circular (Caesar) cipher.** If the text was encoded by shifting each letter forward by `k`, decoding shifts it back by `k` with wrap-around. Reduce `k` first with `k % 26`, and add 26 before taking the final modulus so the result is never negative.

```java
static String decode(String s, int k) {
    int shift = ((k % 26) + 26) % 26;
    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(s.length());
    for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
        if (c >= 'a' && c <= 'z')      sb.append((char) ('a' + (c - 'a' - shift + 26) % 26));
        else if (c >= 'A' && c <= 'Z') sb.append((char) ('A' + (c - 'A' - shift + 26) % 26));
        else                           sb.append(c);   // leave digits and symbols unchanged
    }
    return sb.toString();
}
```

This is O(n) time and O(n) space. Ask whether the direction of the shift is forward or backward in the encoder, and whether digits or other characters also rotate (if digits rotate, use modulus 10 for them).

**Custom sorting with a Comparator.** Keep the class immutable and give it a constructor, then sort with a comparator that has a deterministic tie-breaker:

```java
class Record {
    private final String name; private final int score;
    Record(String name, int score) { this.name = name; this.score = score; }
    String getName() { return name; }  int getScore() { return score; }
}
list.sort(Comparator.comparingInt(Record::getScore).reversed()
                    .thenComparing(Record::getName));
```

Points to mention: use `Integer.compare` (not subtraction, which can overflow) when writing a lambda by hand, a comparator must be consistent (transitive, no contradictions), `List.sort` and `Collections.sort` are stable merge/TimSort variants with O(n log n) time, and `Comparable` defines the natural order while `Comparator` defines external orderings. Finish by testing: empty string, `k = 0`, `k = 26`, `k` larger than 26, mixed case, and ties in the sort key.
